 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>When the factory closed, the town held its breath. For forty years the whistle had woken people at six, and the smell of baking bread had drifted over the rooftops from dawn to dusk. Now the gates were chained, and the silence was louder than any whistle.</p><p>Some families packed up and left for the city. Others refused to give up. Mrs Dlamini, who had worked on the factory floor for twenty-two years, began baking bread in her own kitchen and selling it from a table outside her gate. Within a month there was a queue. Within a year she had hired six of her old colleagues. "They closed a factory," she told a reporter, "not a town."</p>`, qs:[
  M("\"The town held its breath\" suggests that the people were …","anxious and waiting to see what would happen",["breathing exercises together","holding a party","very relaxed"],"Personification of the town."),
  M("\"The silence was louder than any whistle\" is an example of …","a paradox",["a simile","alliteration","onomatopoeia"],"It seems contradictory but makes a point."),
  M("What did the factory produce?","bread",["cars","clothes","whistles"],"'The smell of baking bread…'"),
  M("How long had Mrs Dlamini worked at the factory?","twenty-two years",["forty years","six years","one year"],"Stated in paragraph 2."),
  M("What does \"Within a month there was a queue\" show?","Her bread quickly became popular.",["People were angry with her.","The factory reopened.","She stopped baking."],"Customers lined up."),
  M("What does Mrs Dlamini mean by \"They closed a factory, not a town\"?","The community can survive and rebuild without the factory.",["The town was also closed.","The factory will reopen soon.","She wants to leave town."],"Resilience.")]},
 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>Social media can be a powerful tool. It lets us keep in touch with family overseas, find information instantly and share our ideas with the world. Young activists have used it to start campaigns that changed school policies and even national laws.</p><p>However, it has a darker side. Studies show that teenagers who spend more than three hours a day on social media are more likely to feel anxious and lonely. Algorithms are designed to keep us scrolling, and comparing ourselves to carefully edited photos can damage our self-esteem. The answer is not to delete every app, but to take control: switch off notifications, set time limits and remember that a screen is a window, not a mirror.</p>`, qs:[
  M("According to paragraph 1, which is a benefit of social media?","keeping in touch with family overseas",["feeling lonely","being anxious","losing sleep"],"Stated in paragraph 1."),
  M("Which word signals a change of direction in the argument?","However",["Studies","Algorithms","Young"],"It introduces the counter-argument."),
  M("Why, according to the passage, do people keep scrolling?","Algorithms are designed to keep them scrolling.",["They have nothing else to do.","Their parents tell them to.","It is required at school."],"Stated in paragraph 2."),
  M("What does the writer suggest teenagers should do?","take control by setting limits and switching off notifications",["delete every app","spend more time online","ignore their self-esteem"],"Balanced advice."),
  M("\"A screen is a window, not a mirror\" means …","social media should show you the world, not define how you see yourself",["you should clean your screen","screens break easily","mirrors are better than windows"],"A metaphor."),
  M("The tone of the passage is …","balanced and advisory",["angry and aggressive","humorous","sad and hopeless"],"It weighs both sides.")]},
 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>Grandpa Joe kept a wooden box under his bed, and nobody was allowed to touch it. For years Ayesha imagined what was inside: gold coins, secret letters, perhaps a map to buried treasure.</p><p>On her fifteenth birthday he called her into his room and placed the box in her hands. Inside, wrapped in a faded cloth, was a single pair of worn leather boots. "I walked from Malawi to Johannesburg in these," he said quietly, "with nothing but a loaf of bread and a promise to my mother that I would make something of myself." Ayesha looked at the cracked leather for a long time. It was, she realised, worth more than any treasure she had imagined.</p>`, qs:[
  M("What did Ayesha imagine was in the box?","gold coins, secret letters or a treasure map",["old boots","bread","photographs"],"Stated in paragraph 1."),
  M("When did Grandpa Joe give Ayesha the box?","on her fifteenth birthday",["on his birthday","at Christmas","when he moved house"],"Stated in paragraph 2."),
  M("What was actually in the box?","a pair of worn leather boots",["gold coins","a map","a loaf of bread"],"Wrapped in cloth."),
  M("Where did Grandpa Joe walk from?","Malawi",["Mozambique","Zimbabwe","Lesotho"],"'I walked from Malawi to Johannesburg…'"),
  M("The contrast between what Ayesha expected and what she found is an example of …","situational irony",["alliteration","hyperbole","onomatopoeia"],"The opposite of what was expected."),
  M("What is the theme of the story?","the value of sacrifice and family history",["the importance of wealth","the dangers of travel","fashion"],"The boots meant more than gold.")]}
